WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.980 --> 00:00:04.130
Big expensive cities have
a lot going for them.

00:00:04.130 --> 00:00:06.070
But if you live in one,
you've probably noticed

00:00:06.070 --> 00:00:07.550
that these big expensive cities

00:00:07.550 --> 00:00:10.410
keep getting more, well, expensive.

00:00:10.410 --> 00:00:11.610
You could always move away.

00:00:11.610 --> 00:00:13.150
You might have just
started working remotely.

00:00:13.150 --> 00:00:15.400
So there's really nothing
keeping you there.

00:00:15.400 --> 00:00:17.543
But then again, moving is a hassle.

00:00:18.540 --> 00:00:21.460
Well, what if someone paid
you 10 grand to do it?

00:00:21.460 --> 00:00:23.260
What if they threw in free office space,

00:00:23.260 --> 00:00:25.590
a bicycle or a gym membership?

00:00:25.590 --> 00:00:27.590
Programs and cities and
states around the US

00:00:27.590 --> 00:00:29.190
are doing exactly that,

00:00:29.190 --> 00:00:31.240
and they're getting a lot of interest.

00:00:31.240 --> 00:00:33.180
We've had over 50,000 applications.

00:00:33.180 --> 00:00:35.650
About 90,000 applications.

00:00:35.650 --> 00:00:37.760
We've had over 1800 applicants

00:00:37.760 --> 00:00:39.920
from all over North America.

00:00:39.920 --> 00:00:41.420
So are these nascent programs

00:00:41.420 --> 00:00:43.320
actually beneficial to their communities

00:00:43.320 --> 00:00:44.780
and the workers joining them,

00:00:44.780 --> 00:00:47.440
or are they little more than
flashy advertising campaigns

00:00:47.440 --> 00:00:50.040
for places looking for an
injection of fresh talent?

00:00:57.750 --> 00:00:59.920
Millions of Americans migrate every year,

00:00:59.920 --> 00:01:02.360
but the coronavirus pandemic
saw many more people

00:01:02.360 --> 00:01:05.000
move out from urban
areas into smaller cities

00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:07.960
as remote work allowed them
to do their jobs from home.

00:01:07.960 --> 00:01:10.140
In fact, more than 7 million households

00:01:10.140 --> 00:01:12.200
moved to a different county in 2020.

00:01:12.200 --> 00:01:14.960
That's half a million
more than the year before.

00:01:14.960 --> 00:01:17.050
Smaller cities across
the US are seeing this

00:01:17.050 --> 00:01:20.080
as an opportunity to
attract lifelong residents.

00:01:20.080 --> 00:01:21.510
The remote incentive programs

00:01:21.510 --> 00:01:23.650
that we're seeing pop
up around the country

00:01:23.650 --> 00:01:25.500
all go a little bit like this.

00:01:25.500 --> 00:01:29.830
A city that's looking to lure
new workers from other cities

00:01:29.830 --> 00:01:33.850
offers a monetary incentive
or a cultural incentive

00:01:33.850 --> 00:01:35.270
for people to move.

00:01:35.270 --> 00:01:37.520
Programs like this have
exploded in popularity

00:01:37.520 --> 00:01:39.560
around the US over the last few years,

00:01:39.560 --> 00:01:41.340
and their offerings are
getting more diverse

00:01:41.340 --> 00:01:44.070
and headline-grabbing as
the pandemic prolongs.

00:01:44.070 --> 00:01:47.100
The richest cash offer
comes out of West Virginia.

00:01:47.100 --> 00:01:50.210
So there's a little over
$20,000 that they're offering,

00:01:50.210 --> 00:01:54.900
12,000 in cash and 8,000
in all kinds of amenities.

00:01:54.900 --> 00:01:57.370
So park membership, equipment rental.

00:01:57.370 --> 00:01:58.950
They're really trying to connect people

00:01:58.950 --> 00:02:01.720
to the outdoor wonders of West Virginia

00:02:01.720 --> 00:02:04.010
and they're using the
incentive to do that.

00:02:04.010 --> 00:02:06.010
Offers like Greensburg, Indiana,

00:02:06.010 --> 00:02:07.720
they're offering $5,000 of cash

00:02:07.720 --> 00:02:09.950
but really the richness
comes in their ability

00:02:09.950 --> 00:02:12.020
to sort of connect you to the community.

00:02:12.020 --> 00:02:14.500
They offer things like a
"Grandparents On Demand"

00:02:14.500 --> 00:02:18.130
where a local couple will
babysit your kids for date night

00:02:18.130 --> 00:02:21.630
or stand in for them at
Grandparents Day at school.

00:02:21.630 --> 00:02:22.550
That's Evan Hock.

00:02:22.550 --> 00:02:25.560
He co-founded MakeMyMove.com
at the end of 2020

00:02:25.560 --> 00:02:27.450
to help those curious about moving,

00:02:27.450 --> 00:02:31.080
just as the recent uptick in
remote work was taking off.

00:02:31.080 --> 00:02:33.130
We rolled it out
nationally about a year ago

00:02:33.130 --> 00:02:35.850
where we've had over
40,000 people register

00:02:35.850 --> 00:02:37.930
and are sort of actively
planning their moves

00:02:37.930 --> 00:02:39.430
on the platform.

00:02:39.430 --> 00:02:41.200
As cities promote their best attributes,

00:02:41.200 --> 00:02:44.100
their incentive offerings are
also getting more creative.

00:02:44.100 --> 00:02:46.620
How does $1,000 at Jimmy John's sound?

00:02:46.620 --> 00:02:50.180
Or some free plots of land,
or are free whitewater rafting

00:02:50.180 --> 00:02:52.340
or lift tickets more your speed?

00:02:52.340 --> 00:02:54.320
Now for the first time
in the country's history,

00:02:54.320 --> 00:02:55.950
there are tens of millions of people

00:02:55.950 --> 00:02:57.410
that are geographical free agents.

00:02:57.410 --> 00:03:00.060
They're looking for new homes,

00:03:00.060 --> 00:03:02.710
and that just means it's open season

00:03:02.710 --> 00:03:04.944
for communities that are trying to grow.

00:03:04.944 --> 00:03:06.830
Before digging into these programs,

00:03:06.830 --> 00:03:08.180
let's step back and ask

00:03:08.180 --> 00:03:10.263
what problem are they trying to solve?

00:03:11.410 --> 00:03:13.380
For the most part, they want highly paid,

00:03:13.380 --> 00:03:16.590
highly skilled workers
to join their ranks.

00:03:16.590 --> 00:03:18.250
It's a bit of a different model

00:03:18.250 --> 00:03:19.810
than cities used to be using

00:03:19.810 --> 00:03:24.540
where they were incentivizing
with tax breaks or kickbacks

00:03:24.540 --> 00:03:26.140
to hold companies to move.

00:03:26.140 --> 00:03:28.710
People would want an Amazon
to move into their town

00:03:28.710 --> 00:03:31.380
so they could employ a
lot of local workers.

00:03:31.380 --> 00:03:32.390
Well, it makes sense.

00:03:32.390 --> 00:03:33.950
They have the west coast Washington

00:03:33.950 --> 00:03:36.030
and the east coast Washington.

00:03:36.030 --> 00:03:40.490
Alexa, why should Amazon
build HQ2 in Frisco?

00:03:40.490 --> 00:03:42.240
Frisco, Texas thinks big.

00:03:42.240 --> 00:03:44.330
We have blown up our regulatory process

00:03:44.330 --> 00:03:45.501
over the last couple years

00:03:45.501 --> 00:03:48.800
so that people who've come
to do business with us

00:03:48.800 --> 00:03:50.600
know that we are open for business.

00:03:50.600 --> 00:03:52.610
And this is a kind of
a twist on that model

00:03:52.610 --> 00:03:55.340
where cities are looking
at individual workers

00:03:55.340 --> 00:03:57.400
that aren't necessarily gonna be employed

00:03:57.400 --> 00:03:58.350
in the local economy,

00:03:58.350 --> 00:04:00.610
but are going to be
ticking their salaries,

00:04:00.610 --> 00:04:03.630
spending at local
restaurants, buying homes,

00:04:03.630 --> 00:04:07.820
paying taxes, and bringing
their kids and their partners

00:04:07.820 --> 00:04:10.390
and maybe their family members.

00:04:10.390 --> 00:04:12.280
You can find the nation's busiest program

00:04:12.280 --> 00:04:13.850
in Tulsa, Oklahoma.

00:04:13.850 --> 00:04:17.190
Tulsa started in 2018 before the pandemic,

00:04:17.190 --> 00:04:21.410
before the remote work way
of life became so widespread.

00:04:21.410 --> 00:04:24.550
So they started with about 75 people.

00:04:24.550 --> 00:04:26.867
They've grown exponentially
over the past few years.

00:04:26.867 --> 00:04:29.780
But what they realized
is there's a big problem

00:04:29.780 --> 00:04:31.410
with brain drain in Tulsa.

00:04:31.410 --> 00:04:34.440
A lot of people go to universities
in the region and leave.

00:04:34.440 --> 00:04:37.190
So what Tulsa was trying to
do is stem that brain drain.

00:04:37.190 --> 00:04:39.943
There's over 1300 people
in Tulsa remote now.

00:04:39.943 --> 00:04:41.450
And we really are looking for people

00:04:41.450 --> 00:04:42.750
that don't necessarily have a mentality

00:04:42.750 --> 00:04:44.770
of I'm coming to change a city.

00:04:44.770 --> 00:04:47.570
More so, they wanna be a
part of something awesome

00:04:47.570 --> 00:04:48.403
that's already happening.

00:04:48.403 --> 00:04:51.330
We have a weekly newsletter
that goes out to our members

00:04:51.330 --> 00:04:53.290
that highlights all the
different social events

00:04:53.290 --> 00:04:54.980
and gatherings that we have.

00:04:54.980 --> 00:04:58.270
We've got a slack community
that we post updates in there.

00:04:58.270 --> 00:05:00.070
Oftentimes things are
happening organically

00:05:00.070 --> 00:05:03.460
as well with members, 90%
of the folks that are done

00:05:03.460 --> 00:05:05.900
with their one year
commitment stay in Tulsa

00:05:05.900 --> 00:05:06.833
beyond that year.

00:05:07.720 --> 00:05:09.800
Some programs like Northwest Arkansas's

00:05:09.800 --> 00:05:11.630
Bitcoin and a bike incentive

00:05:11.630 --> 00:05:14.270
are designed not for pulling
in thousands of new residents,

00:05:14.270 --> 00:05:15.900
but to deliberately select those

00:05:15.900 --> 00:05:18.200
with a set of specific qualities.

00:05:18.200 --> 00:05:21.600
Today, we've got about
50 people on the ground

00:05:21.600 --> 00:05:24.240
that have taken advantage
of the incentive program.

00:05:24.240 --> 00:05:25.950
How to tell your spouse
you're considering a move

00:05:25.950 --> 00:05:27.300
to Northwest Arkansas?

00:05:27.300 --> 00:05:29.360
Step one, remind them of your vows.

00:05:29.360 --> 00:05:30.760
What we're trying to do is identify

00:05:30.760 --> 00:05:32.440
the right types of talent.

00:05:32.440 --> 00:05:34.990
And the reason why is we've have

00:05:34.990 --> 00:05:37.130
one of the strongest
economies in the country

00:05:37.130 --> 00:05:41.280
but it's built primarily
on Walmart, Tyson,

00:05:41.280 --> 00:05:43.440
J.B. Hunt there at 14

00:05:43.440 --> 00:05:47.040
hundred companies with presence
here that supply Walmart.

00:05:47.040 --> 00:05:50.860
Right now, they are
probably, they're successful.

00:05:50.860 --> 00:05:52.330
They're gonna continue to be successful,

00:05:52.330 --> 00:05:55.540
but they're not gonna hire
a lot more workers, right?

00:05:55.540 --> 00:05:58.680
Every region, they need more tech talent.

00:05:58.680 --> 00:06:01.210
They need more expertise in blockchain.

00:06:01.210 --> 00:06:04.880
So, this program is to really
define those key people

00:06:04.880 --> 00:06:07.760
that are gonna help move
this region forward.

00:06:07.760 --> 00:06:10.080
For Hawaii's program, Movers &amp; Shakas,

00:06:10.080 --> 00:06:13.053
the goal isn't necessarily
growing the population at all.

00:06:14.300 --> 00:06:15.160
We are very focused

00:06:15.160 --> 00:06:17.440
on trying to bring back
return in Kamaʻāina,

00:06:17.440 --> 00:06:20.200
which are people who have lived in Hawaii

00:06:20.200 --> 00:06:24.300
typically refers to people
who are born and raised here,

00:06:24.300 --> 00:06:26.000
which is very different,

00:06:26.000 --> 00:06:29.320
which can include native
Hawaiians, but not necessarily.

00:06:29.320 --> 00:06:33.150
Our program is really about
bringing people to Hawaii

00:06:33.150 --> 00:06:35.820
to learn, contribute and connect.

00:06:35.820 --> 00:06:38.010
A goal of our program isn't necessarily

00:06:38.010 --> 00:06:41.470
to have people move to Hawaii
or move back to Hawaii,

00:06:41.470 --> 00:06:42.910
although that would be awesome,

00:06:42.910 --> 00:06:44.919
and we have a couple examples of that,

00:06:44.919 --> 00:06:48.460
but it's really to build
long lasting personal

00:06:48.460 --> 00:06:51.770
and professional ties and
relationships to Hawaii.

00:06:51.770 --> 00:06:55.480
And what that does is
reduce the economic distance

00:06:55.480 --> 00:06:56.633
with the continent.

00:06:57.640 --> 00:06:59.060
Aside from people taking advantage

00:06:59.060 --> 00:07:00.440
of the programs themselves,

00:07:00.440 --> 00:07:03.013
their mere existence makes
for good advertising.

00:07:05.560 --> 00:07:09.033
It's a climb that's
been started by so many,

00:07:10.050 --> 00:07:13.853
a climb taken by those who
seek a life less ordinary.

00:07:14.780 --> 00:07:16.580
Maybe you already wanted to move

00:07:16.580 --> 00:07:19.570
and maybe already your mom
lives in Savannah, Georgia,

00:07:19.570 --> 00:07:21.300
but you never thought it would be possible

00:07:21.300 --> 00:07:22.430
to make that kind of move.

00:07:22.430 --> 00:07:24.430
And then you see Savannah, Georgia

00:07:24.430 --> 00:07:26.710
putting together this kind of package

00:07:26.710 --> 00:07:29.030
and it gets back on your radar.

00:07:29.030 --> 00:07:30.040
And for a lot of the people

00:07:30.040 --> 00:07:31.980
that these programs are luring,

00:07:31.980 --> 00:07:36.340
the $10,000 or $5,000 or
whatever the case may be,

00:07:36.340 --> 00:07:38.930
is maybe covering just the moving cost

00:07:38.930 --> 00:07:42.450
but isn't enough to make
you uproot your entire life

00:07:42.450 --> 00:07:43.980
and move somewhere new.

00:07:43.980 --> 00:07:45.590
So far, all of these programs

00:07:45.590 --> 00:07:48.040
are relatively small
compared to the populations

00:07:48.040 --> 00:07:49.860
of the places in which they exist.

00:07:49.860 --> 00:07:52.930
So while they haven't caused
too much fuss with locals...

00:07:52.930 --> 00:07:56.430
There's traffic jams now on
some of the main arterials.

00:07:56.430 --> 00:07:57.790
We're just not used to that.

00:07:57.790 --> 00:08:00.750
So our patience level is not quite there.

00:08:00.750 --> 00:08:02.120
There is always going to be

00:08:02.120 --> 00:08:05.480
a healthy amount of skepticism
to the arrival of newcomers.

00:08:05.480 --> 00:08:09.260
Tulsa's program is funded
with philanthropic dollars.

00:08:09.260 --> 00:08:11.440
It's funded by the George
Kaiser Family Foundation

00:08:11.440 --> 00:08:14.470
which is this big
philanthropy based in town.

00:08:14.470 --> 00:08:16.420
That's the same for Northwest Arkansas,

00:08:17.340 --> 00:08:20.220
which is funded by another philanthropy

00:08:20.220 --> 00:08:21.420
that's locally based.

00:08:21.420 --> 00:08:24.120
It seems like that's the
dominant model so far,

00:08:24.120 --> 00:08:26.800
but some taxpayers have
started to put the bill

00:08:26.800 --> 00:08:28.020
in other cities.

00:08:28.020 --> 00:08:30.750
It's usually easier to
make the sell to the public

00:08:30.750 --> 00:08:32.750
that you're giving money to outsiders

00:08:32.750 --> 00:08:34.450
when it's not taxpayer money

00:08:34.450 --> 00:08:35.810
funding these kinds of programs.

00:08:35.810 --> 00:08:37.260
In Northwest Arkansas,

00:08:37.260 --> 00:08:40.440
there's a really large urban-rural divide.

00:08:40.440 --> 00:08:42.200
There are a lot of hourly workers.

00:08:42.200 --> 00:08:44.847
They're paying tech workers, entrepreneurs

00:08:44.847 --> 00:08:48.230
$10,000 in Bitcoin to move there.

00:08:48.230 --> 00:08:49.410
There is the sense among some

00:08:49.410 --> 00:08:50.920
that the money could be better spent

00:08:50.920 --> 00:08:52.290
incentivizing other things,

00:08:52.290 --> 00:08:55.690
like asking businesses to
raise their minimum wage.

00:08:55.690 --> 00:08:58.560
I myself was a skeptic.

00:08:58.560 --> 00:09:02.160
I actually got this
role because of an op-ed

00:09:02.160 --> 00:09:03.980
that I wrote in the Star Advertiser,

00:09:03.980 --> 00:09:06.520
which is our local daily newspaper

00:09:06.520 --> 00:09:09.100
which was largely against the program.

00:09:09.100 --> 00:09:12.390
I was like, why are we
bringing in tech bros

00:09:12.390 --> 00:09:16.040
dripping with COVID to
come and drive my rent up

00:09:16.040 --> 00:09:18.060
and destroy the aloha spirit?

00:09:18.060 --> 00:09:21.360
When it came to Hawaii,
I felt very protective.

00:09:21.360 --> 00:09:24.540
What I realized is that
that us versus them

00:09:24.540 --> 00:09:28.490
kind of tribal mentality is
very real and it's innate.

00:09:28.490 --> 00:09:30.803
And we have to acknowledge
that it's there.

00:09:31.660 --> 00:09:34.050
And we also have to ask ourselves like,

00:09:34.050 --> 00:09:36.440
well, how do we bring in newcomers?

00:09:36.440 --> 00:09:40.173
How do we see things
as not a zero sum game?

00:09:41.610 --> 00:09:43.060
Tulsa Remote's Justin Harlan

00:09:43.060 --> 00:09:44.140
admits that the program

00:09:44.140 --> 00:09:46.250
had some critics from the local community,

00:09:46.250 --> 00:09:48.820
but a recent independent
economic impact report

00:09:48.820 --> 00:09:51.300
estimates its brought in $62 million

00:09:51.300 --> 00:09:54.963
in local earnings into the
city, and added 600 jobs.

00:09:56.100 --> 00:10:00.550
For many decades, we've
seen export of talent

00:10:00.550 --> 00:10:02.110
out of a lot of communities

00:10:02.110 --> 00:10:04.330
towards these big economic centers.

00:10:04.330 --> 00:10:06.230
And so one way to think about this

00:10:06.230 --> 00:10:09.623
is a redistribution of a
lot of that economic impact.

00:10:10.560 --> 00:10:12.250
Of course, moving between cities

00:10:12.250 --> 00:10:14.120
can be a huge lifestyle change,

00:10:14.120 --> 00:10:16.480
and it's not necessarily for everyone.

00:10:16.480 --> 00:10:17.610
For a few weeks ago,

00:10:17.610 --> 00:10:20.830
it was literally snowing in the backyard.

00:10:20.830 --> 00:10:24.340
Listen, I moved to Las Vegas
not to have snow like that.

00:10:24.340 --> 00:10:26.852
Why I'm leaving Austin, Texas,

00:10:26.852 --> 00:10:30.370
it's really because of
the lack of diversity.

00:10:30.370 --> 00:10:32.820
I mean, also in a lot of Facebook groups,

00:10:32.820 --> 00:10:35.030
and like all through the Facebook groups

00:10:35.030 --> 00:10:37.693
who are like the dating here is trash.

00:10:41.080 --> 00:10:42.780
As for those big expensive cities

00:10:42.780 --> 00:10:44.460
bleeding remote worker talent,

00:10:44.460 --> 00:10:46.610
there doesn't seem to
be much to worry about.

00:10:46.610 --> 00:10:49.220
Between March 2020 and February 2021,

00:10:49.220 --> 00:10:53.400
84% of movers in the US
stayed in the same metro area.

00:10:53.400 --> 00:10:55.200
In New York City, the most popular place

00:10:55.200 --> 00:10:58.343
people in Manhattan moved to, Brooklyn.

00:11:00.630 --> 00:11:03.050
In the end, even if you
personally don't take part in

00:11:03.050 --> 00:11:05.820
or have any interest whatsoever
in one of these programs,

00:11:05.820 --> 00:11:07.610
there is an advantage for these places

00:11:07.610 --> 00:11:09.550
to get their names out there.

00:11:09.550 --> 00:11:10.980
I think for a lot of cities,

00:11:10.980 --> 00:11:12.730
they think there's nothing really to lose

00:11:12.730 --> 00:11:15.840
despite some of the
questions about its efficacy.

00:11:15.840 --> 00:11:19.963
It doesn't really hurt to
say we're open to newcomers.

00:11:22.002 --> 00:11:24.152
We're a place that we
wanna put on the map.

